---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { COMPANY } from '../data/apps';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`About — ${COMPANY.name}`}
  description="Nintek is a one-person studio built around a real life — an SRE career, a woodworking hobby, a health journey, daily games, and keeping a home."
  path="/about"
>
  <section class="about-hero container reveal">
    <div class="hero-inner">
      <div class="hero-text">
        <span class="eyebrow">
          <span class="eyebrow-dot"></span>
          The maker behind the apps
        </span>
        <h1>
          Built for the life
          <span class="em">I'm actually living.</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="about-lead">
          I'm an SRE by trade, a woodworker by weekend, and someone who went
          looking for software that fit my actual life — not a sanitized version
          of it. When I couldn't find it, I built it. That's Nintek.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-photo">
        <img src="/maker.png" alt="Enzo, the maker behind Nintek" width="220" height="220" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="container">
    <div class="about-story">

      <section class="story-section reveal">
        <span class="story-eyebrow">Work</span>
        <h2>An SRE who lives in terminals and docs.</h2>
        <p>
          My day job is keeping systems running. That means notes — a lot of
          them. Incident timelines, runbook tweaks, things I figured out at
          2am that I absolutely cannot lose. I tried every notes app and none
          of them felt right: too cluttered, too eager to sync everything to
          someone else's cloud, too slow to open when I'm already in the
          middle of something.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/tabloom">Tabloom</a> became the notebook, <a href="/pulsewire">PulseWire</a>
          became the evidence-linked reader, and <a href="/prism">Prism</a> became
          the focused AI workbench. <a href="/lantern">Lantern</a> holds long-form
          learning while <a href="/cairn">Cairn</a> stays focused on certification.
        </p>
        <div class="story-apps">
          <a href="/tabloom" class="story-app-pill">Tabloom</a>
          <a href="/pulsewire" class="story-app-pill">PulseWire</a>
          <a href="/prism" class="story-app-pill">Prism</a>
          <a href="/lantern" class="story-app-pill">Lantern</a>
          <a href="/cairn" class="story-app-pill">Cairn</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="story-section reveal">
        <span class="story-eyebrow">Craft</span>
        <h2>A hobbyist with a disorganized shop.</h2>
        <p>
          I've been doing woodworking long enough to have a real shop — table
          saw, router table, the works. And long enough to know the frustration
          of starting a project without being able to answer basic questions:
          do I have enough walnut for this? Where did I put the pocket hole
          jig? What were the measurements on that last cut list?
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/workshop">Workshop</a> is a build log — projects, cut lists,
          material counts, the notes I used to scribble on scrap wood and then
          lose. <a href="/shopkeep">ShopKeep</a> is the inventory layer: what's
          in the shop, where it lives, and when I'm running low on something.
          Together they're the shop foreman I never had.
        </p>
        <div class="story-apps">
          <a href="/workshop" class="story-app-pill">Workshop</a>
          <a href="/shopkeep" class="story-app-pill">ShopKeep</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="story-section reveal">
        <span class="story-eyebrow">Health</span>
        <h2>When the numbers started to matter.</h2>
        <p>
          A while back, I started a new health chapter — the kind where you
          suddenly have metrics to track that you'd never paid attention to
          before. Weight trends, doses, how you're actually feeling week over
          week. I went looking for an app to help me stay on top of it and
          found the same thing everywhere: designs that felt clinical, privacy
          policies that read like a data harvest, and UX clearly made for
          someone who wasn't me.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/tare">Tare</a> is the app I wished I had. It tracks the metrics
          that matter on a GLP-1 journey, smooths out the daily noise so you
          can see the actual trend, and keeps everything on the device. The
          iPhone version has no account at all — your history syncs through
          your own private iCloud, and nobody else has a copy.
        </p>
        <div class="story-apps">
          <a href="/tare" class="story-app-pill">Tare</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="story-section reveal">
        <span class="story-eyebrow">Making</span>
        <h2>Tools that escaped the original household hub.</h2>
        <p>
          Some parts of the original household system became clearer when they
          stopped pretending to be one product. <a href="/shapepilot">ShapePilot</a>
          owns approachable 2D and 3D design for fabrication. <a href="/marquee">Marquee</a>
          owns private media-library operations. <a href="/watchtower">Watchtower</a>
          owns infrastructure monitoring and alerting.
        </p>
        <p>
          The split is a product boundary, not a new portal. Each app has its own
          source, identity, and release evidence; <a href="/hearth">Hearth</a> no
          longer markets those extracted tools as rooms inside the current runtime.
        </p>
        <div class="story-apps">
          <a href="/shapepilot" class="story-app-pill">ShapePilot</a>
          <a href="/marquee" class="story-app-pill">Marquee</a>
          <a href="/watchtower" class="story-app-pill">Watchtower</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="story-section reveal">
        <span class="story-eyebrow">Home</span>
        <h2>The household that runs on lost receipts.</h2>
        <p>
          I cook, I do my own maintenance, and I've got a property with
          opinions about when things need attention. The problem is the paper
          trail — or the lack of one. Where's the HVAC filter size? When was
          the last time the water heater was serviced? What was in that
          marinade everyone loved?
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/hearth">Hearth</a> is the current household and property
          fieldbook: maintenance, inventory, yard, garden, pool, recipes, and the
          work that needs attention today. Infrastructure monitoring now belongs
          to <a href="/watchtower">Watchtower</a>; it is not a legacy room inside
          Hearth-v2.
        </p>
        <div class="story-apps">
          <a href="/hearth" class="story-app-pill">Hearth</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="story-section story-native reveal">
        <span class="story-eyebrow">Direction</span>
        <h2>Native status belongs to each product.</h2>
        <p>
          <a href="/tare">Tare</a>, <a href="/shopkeep">ShopKeep</a>, and
          <a href="/cairn">Cairn</a> have native builds with different private or
          blocked release states. <a href="/watchtower">Watchtower</a> has a private
          iOS companion whose retained Hearth identifiers are implementation detail,
          not product naming. Workshop’s native client is archived.
        </p>
        <p>
          None currently has a public App Store listing. The portfolio does not
          infer prices, dates, TestFlight access, or acquisition links from a build
          simply existing.
        </p>
        <div class="story-apps">
          <a href="/ios" class="story-app-pill story-app-pill--strong">
            The native apps →
          </a>
          <a href="/ios#cairn" class="story-app-pill">Cairn</a>
          <a href="/ios#shopkeep" class="story-app-pill">ShopKeep</a>
          <a href="/ios#tare" class="story-app-pill">Tare</a>
          <a href="/ios#watchtower" class="story-app-pill">Watchtower</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="story-section platform-policy reveal" id="platform-policy">
        <h2>Web and native are not the same promise.</h2>
        <p>
          Installability and offline behavior are product decisions, not a
          portfolio-wide badge. These boundaries are the current public policy:
        </p>
        <dl class="platform-decisions">
          <div>
            <dt><a href="/tare">Tare</a></dt>
            <dd>
              The browser product is retired. Production web traffic is relay-only
              for optional native AI features; there is no Tare PWA, offline web
              app, browser sign-in, or web background-reminder claim.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt><a href="/workshop">Workshop</a></dt>
            <dd>
              The web workspace is online and account-backed, not an installable or
              offline PWA. It is the supported Workshop client.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt><a href="/cairn">Cairn</a></dt>
            <dd>
              The web workspace is online and account-backed. CairnNative is a
              separate offline product with private iCloud sync; the two products
              never share progress or account data.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </section>

      <section class="story-close reveal">
        <p>
          Nintek is a one-person studio. Every app is private by default,
          built to last, and made because I needed it first. If any of this
          resonates — or if you've got something you're missing —
          <a href={`mailto:${COMPANY.email}`}>get in touch</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about-hero {
    padding-top: 80px;
    padding-bottom: 72px;
  }

  .hero-inner {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 3rem;
    max-width: 72ch;
  }

  .hero-text {
    flex: 1 1 0;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .hero-photo {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
  }

  .hero-photo img {
    width: 198px;
    height: 198px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: center top;
    border: 3px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--copper) 30%, transparent);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-float);
  }

  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .hero-inner {
      flex-direction: column-reverse;
      align-items: flex-start;
      gap: 1.5rem;
    }
    .hero-photo img {
      width: 132px;
      height: 132px;
    }
  }

  .about-hero h1 {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.25rem);
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .about-hero h1 .em {
    color: var(--copper);
    display: block;
  }

  .about-lead {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    max-width: 60ch;
  }

  .about-story {
    max-width: 68ch;
    padding-bottom: 120px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0;
  }

  .story-section {
    padding-top: 56px;
    padding-bottom: 56px;
    border-top: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 10%, transparent);
  }

  .story-eyebrow {
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--copper);
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .story-section h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.3rem, 3vw, 1.75rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }

  .story-section p {
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.72;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .story-section p:last-of-type {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .story-section p a {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 500;
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
    text-decoration-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--copper) 40%, transparent);
    transition: text-decoration-color 0.15s;
  }

  .story-section p a:hover {
    text-decoration-color: var(--copper);
  }

  .story-apps {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .story-app-pill {
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    padding: 0.3rem 0.75rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--copper) 10%, transparent);
    color: var(--copper);
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--copper) 25%, transparent);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: background 0.15s, border-color 0.15s;
  }

  .story-app-pill:hover {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--copper) 18%, transparent);
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--copper) 45%, transparent);
  }

  .story-app-pill--strong {
    background: var(--copper);
    color: #fff;
    border-color: var(--copper);
  }

  .story-app-pill--strong:hover {
    background: var(--accent);
    border-color: var(--accent);
  }

  .story-native p strong {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .platform-policy > p {
    max-width: 62ch;
  }

  .platform-policy {
    scroll-margin-top: 120px;
  }

  .platform-decisions {
    margin: 2rem 0 0;
    border-top: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 12%, transparent);
  }

  .platform-decisions > div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(7rem, 0.28fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 1.5rem;
    padding: 1.25rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 12%, transparent);
  }

  .platform-decisions dt,
  .platform-decisions dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .platform-decisions dt {
    font-family: var(--font-brand);
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .platform-decisions dd {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    line-height: 1.72;
  }

  .platform-decisions a {
    color: var(--ink);
    text-decoration-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--copper) 40%, transparent);
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .platform-decisions > div {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 0.4rem;
    }
  }

  .story-close {
    padding-top: 56px;
    border-top: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 10%, transparent);
  }

  .story-close p {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.72;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .story-close a {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 500;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }
</style>
